<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="mth124-act-average-rate-of-change">

<page>
<assemblage xml:id="aroc-act-review">
<title>Average Rate of Change</title>
<p>
The <alert>average rate of change</alert> of a function <m>f</m> from <m>x=a</m> to <m>x=b</m> is
<me>\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a},</me>
the change in output divided by the change in input. Three things to keep in mind. Only the two endpoints matter, no matter what happens in between. The units are always output units per input unit. A negative answer means the output decreased across the interval, not that the function was negative.
</p>
</assemblage>

<paragraphs>
<title>Rate of Change from a Formula</title>
<example xml:id="aroc-act-example-formula">
<title>Worked Example</title>
<statement>
<p>Find the average rate of change of <m>\displaystyle h(x) = \frac{60}{x}</m> on the interval <m>[3,10]</m>.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Evaluate at both endpoints first, then divide.
<me>h(10) = \frac{60}{10} = 6, \qquad h(3) = \frac{60}{3} = 20, \qquad \frac{h(10)-h(3)}{10-3} = \frac{6-20}{7} = \frac{-14}{7} = -2.</me>
</p>
<p>The average rate of change is <m>-2</m>. On average, the output drops by <m>2</m> units for each <m>1</m> unit increase in the input across this interval.</p>
</solution>
</example>
</paragraphs>

<exercise xml:id="aroc-act-rational" workspace="3cm">
<statement>
<p>Find the average rate of change of <m>\displaystyle g(x) = \frac{36}{x}</m> on the interval <m>[2,6]</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>-3</m></p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p><m>g(2) = 18</m> and <m>g(6) = 6</m>, so the average rate of change is <m>\dfrac{6-18}{6-2} = \dfrac{-12}{4} = -3</m>.</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="aroc-act-quadratic" workspace="3.4cm">
<statement>
<p>Find the average rate of change of <m>k(x) = 2x^2 - 5</m> on the interval <m>[-1,3]</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>4</m></p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>
<m>k(-1) = 2(1) - 5 = -3</m> and <m>k(3) = 2(9) - 5 = 13</m>, so
<me>\frac{13-(-3)}{3-(-1)} = \frac{16}{4} = 4.</me>
</p>
<p>Watch the denominator here; students often write <m>3-1</m> instead of <m>3-(-1)</m>.</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="aroc-act-radical" workspace="3cm">
<statement>
<p>Find the average rate of change of <m>m(x) = 12\sqrt{x}</m> on the interval <m>[1,9]</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>3</m></p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p><m>m(1) = 12</m> and <m>m(9) = 36</m>, so <m>\dfrac{36-12}{9-1} = \dfrac{24}{8} = 3</m>.</p>
</solution>
</exercise>
</page>

<page>
<paragraphs>
<title>Rate of Change from a Table</title>
<example xml:id="aroc-act-example-table">
<title>Worked Example</title>
<statement>
<p>A new app is gaining subscribers. The total number of subscribers <m>N(x)</m> after <m>x</m> weeks is given below. Find the average rate of change from <m>x=3</m> to <m>x=12</m> and interpret it.</p>
<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor" right="minor">
<row>
<cell right="major"><m>x</m> (weeks)</cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>3</m></cell>
<cell><m>6</m></cell>
<cell><m>9</m></cell>
<cell><m>12</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell right="major"><m>N(x)</m> (subscribers)</cell>
<cell><m>120</m></cell>
<cell><m>210</m></cell>
<cell><m>340</m></cell>
<cell><m>480</m></cell>
<cell><m>660</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Read the two endpoint columns, <m>N(3) = 210</m> and <m>N(12) = 660</m>. Then
<me>\frac{N(12)-N(3)}{12-3} = \frac{660-210}{9} = \frac{450}{9} = 50.</me>
</p>
<p>The average rate of change is <m>50</m> subscribers per week. Between week <m>3</m> and week <m>12</m>, the app gained an average of <m>50</m> new subscribers each week. The columns at <m>x=6</m> and <m>x=9</m> never entered the computation.</p>
</solution>
</example>

<p>Questions 4 and 5 both refer to the following table. A large tank is being drained, and <m>W(x)</m> is the amount of water left in the tank after <m>x</m> minutes.</p>
<sidebyside width="75%">
<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor" right="minor">
<row>
<cell right="major"><m>x</m> (minutes)</cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
<cell><m>5</m></cell>
<cell><m>10</m></cell>
<cell><m>15</m></cell>
<cell><m>20</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell right="major"><m>W(x)</m> (gallons)</cell>
<cell><m>800</m></cell>
<cell><m>620</m></cell>
<cell><m>430</m></cell>
<cell><m>290</m></cell>
<cell><m>155</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<exercise xml:id="aroc-act-tank-later">
<introduction>
<p>Consider the interval from <m>x=5</m> to <m>x=20</m>.</p>
</introduction>

<task workspace="2.6cm">
<statement>
<p>Determine the average rate of change of the water amount on this interval, and state its units.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>-31</m> gal/min</p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p><m>\dfrac{155-620}{20-5} = \dfrac{-465}{15} = -31</m> gallons per minute.</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task workspace="2.2cm">
<statement>
<p>Describe the meaning of your result in the context of the question. Write a full sentence that does not use the letters <m>W</m> or <m>x</m>.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>Between <m>5</m> and <m>20</m> minutes after draining began, the tank lost an average of <m>31</m> gallons of water each minute.</p>
</solution>
</task>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="aroc-act-tank-earlier">
<introduction>
<p>Now consider the interval from <m>x=0</m> to <m>x=10</m>.</p>
</introduction>

<task workspace="2.6cm">
<statement>
<p>Determine the average rate of change of the water amount on this interval.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>-37</m> gal/min</p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p><m>\dfrac{430-800}{10-0} = \dfrac{-370}{10} = -37</m> gallons per minute.</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task workspace="2in">
<statement>
<p>Compare your answer to the one from Question 4. During which stretch of time was the tank draining faster? Explain how you can tell from the two numbers.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>The tank drained faster over the first ten minutes. It was losing <m>37</m> gallons per minute there compared with <m>31</m> gallons per minute on the later interval. Both are negative, so the comparison is about which is farther from zero, not which is larger as a signed number. This is a good place to point out that the average rate of change depends on the interval you choose.</p>
</solution>
</task>
</exercise>
</page>

<page>
<paragraphs>
<title>Rate of Change from a Graph</title>
<example xml:id="aroc-act-example-graph">
<title>Worked Example</title>
<statement>
<p>The graph of <m>h(x)</m> is shown below. Find the average rate of change of <m>h</m> from <m>x=0</m> to <m>x=2</m>.</p>
<image width="40%">
<shortdescription>A downward-opening parabola with a dashed line segment joining two points on it.</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>A downward-opening parabola is drawn on a coordinate grid from x equals negative three to x equals three, with its highest point at zero, two. It crosses the horizontal axis at negative two, zero and two, zero. The points zero, two and two, zero are marked, and a dashed line segment connects them.</p>
</description>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="aroc-act-example-graph-h">
<diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
<definition>h(x) = -0.5*x^2 + 2</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-3.5,-3.5,3.5,3.5)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<graph function="h" domain="(-3,3)" stroke="blue" thickness="3" arrows="2"/>
<line endpoints="((0,2),(2,0))" stroke="navy" thickness="2" dash="4 4"/>
<point p="(0,2)" fill="navy" stroke="navy"/>
<point p="(2,0)" fill="navy" stroke="navy"/>
</coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Read the outputs off the graph: <m>h(0) = 2</m> and <m>h(2) = 0</m>. Then
<me>\frac{h(2)-h(0)}{2-0} = \frac{0-2}{2} = -1.</me>
</p>
<p>The average rate of change is <m>-1</m>. Notice that this is exactly the slope of the dashed line drawn through the two points on the curve, which is what the average rate of change measures.</p>
</solution>
</example>
</paragraphs>

<exercise xml:id="aroc-act-graph">
<introduction>
<p>The graph of <m>f(x)</m> is shown below.</p>
<image width="40%">
<shortdescription>An upward-opening parabola with three marked points.</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>An upward-opening parabola is drawn on a coordinate grid from x equals negative three to x equals three, with its lowest point at zero, negative two. The points negative two, zero; zero, negative two; and two, zero are marked on the curve.</p>
</description>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="aroc-act-graph-f">
<diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
<definition>f(x) = 0.5*x^2 - 2</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-3.5,-3.5,3.5,3.5)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<graph function="f" domain="(-3,3)" stroke="blue" thickness="3" arrows="2"/>
<point p="(-2,0)" fill="blue" stroke="blue"/>
<point p="(0,-2)" fill="blue" stroke="blue"/>
<point p="(2,0)" fill="blue" stroke="blue"/>
</coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</introduction>

<task workspace="2.2cm">
<statement>
<p>Find the average rate of change of <m>f</m> from <m>x=-2</m> to <m>x=0</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>-1</m></p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p><m>f(-2) = 0</m> and <m>f(0) = -2</m>, so <m>\dfrac{-2-0}{0-(-2)} = \dfrac{-2}{2} = -1</m>.</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task workspace="2.2cm">
<statement>
<p>Find the average rate of change of <m>f</m> from <m>x=0</m> to <m>x=2</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>1</m></p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p><m>f(0) = -2</m> and <m>f(2) = 0</m>, so <m>\dfrac{0-(-2)}{2-0} = \dfrac{2}{2} = 1</m>.</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task workspace="1.5in">
<statement>
<p>Your two answers have opposite signs even though the graph is one smooth curve. Explain what each sign is telling you about the outputs on that interval.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>The negative answer says the outputs decreased on the way from <m>x=-2</m> to <m>x=0</m>; the positive answer says they increased from <m>x=0</m> to <m>x=2</m>. The curve turns around at the bottom, so the sign of the average rate of change flips depending on which side of the low point you are on.</p>
</solution>
</task>
</exercise>
</page>

<page>
<paragraphs>
<title>Rate of Change in Context</title>
<example xml:id="aroc-act-example-context">
<title>Worked Example</title>
<statement>
<p>Suppose <m>f(t) = 150 - 16t^2</m> is the height in feet of an object dropped from a building after <m>t</m> seconds. Find the average rate of change of <m>f</m> on <m>[0,3]</m> and describe what it means.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Evaluate at both endpoints.
<me>f(0) = 150 - 16(0)^2 = 150, \qquad f(3) = 150 - 16(3)^2 = 150 - 144 = 6.</me>
Then
<me>\frac{f(3)-f(0)}{3-0} = \frac{6-150}{3} = \frac{-144}{3} = -48.</me>
</p>
<p>The average rate of change is <m>-48</m> feet per second. During the first <m>3</m> seconds of the fall, the object drops an average of <m>48</m> feet each second, so its average velocity over that stretch is <m>48</m> feet per second downward.</p>
</solution>
</example>
</paragraphs>

<exercise xml:id="aroc-act-falling">
<introduction>
<p>Suppose <m>s(t) = 300 - 16t^2</m> is the height in feet of an object dropped from a building after <m>t</m> seconds.</p>
</introduction>

<task workspace="3cm">
<statement>
<p>Find the average rate of change of <m>s</m> on <m>[1,4]</m>, and state its units.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>-80</m> ft/s</p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>
<m>s(1) = 300 - 16 = 284</m> and <m>s(4) = 300 - 256 = 44</m>, so
<me>\frac{44-284}{4-1} = \frac{-240}{3} = -80</me>
feet per second.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task workspace="2.2cm">
<statement>
<p>Describe in words what your answer to part (a) means. Your sentence should mention the object, the time, and the direction it is moving.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>Between <m>1</m> and <m>4</m> seconds after the object was dropped, its height decreased by an average of <m>80</m> feet each second, meaning it was moving downward.</p>
</solution>
</task>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="aroc-act-van">
<introduction>
<p>A delivery van is purchased new, and its value in dollars after <m>t</m> years is given by <m>V(t) = 32000 - 2400t</m>.</p>
</introduction>

<task workspace="2.6cm">
<statement>
<p>Find the average rate of change of <m>V</m> on <m>[0,3]</m>, and state its units.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>-2400</m> dollars/yr</p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>
<m>V(0) = 32000</m> and <m>V(3) = 32000 - 7200 = 24800</m>, so
<me>\frac{24800-32000}{3-0} = \frac{-7200}{3} = -2400</me>
dollars per year.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task workspace="2.6cm">
<statement>
<p>Find the average rate of change of <m>V</m> on <m>[5,9]</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>-2400</m> dollars/yr</p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>
<m>V(5) = 20000</m> and <m>V(9) = 10400</m>, so
<me>\frac{10400-20000}{9-5} = \frac{-9600}{4} = -2400</me>
dollars per year.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task workspace="2in">
<statement>
<p>You should have gotten the same answer twice. What is it about this particular function that makes the average rate of change come out the same on every interval?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p><m>V</m> is a linear function, and its slope is <m>-2400</m>. For a line, the average rate of change between any two points is just the slope, so the interval does not matter. For every other function type in this activity, it does.</p>
</solution>
</task>
</exercise>
</page>

</worksheet>
